/*****************************************************************************
    kantGrammar -- the new grammar's starting file (SEQ 436, 2026-10-27).

    A copy of the live region of incant/grammar (the Grokking define), with
    every rule it defines renamed UPPERCASe: uppercase, the last letter
    lowercase. Nothing loads this file yet. Rung 1 reaches it by swapping
    Grokking in the search list for KantGrammar, and is where the actions
    change; here they are as copied. Note that a rule whose C++ action is
    found by its name pattern (aCTion plus the rule name) finds none under
    its new name; explicit ruleMethod and parseAction attributes are kept.

    Shared, referenced by name and never redefined here, so the old
    definitions answer: TOKENize, DEFINing, PRINTing, ANYtoken, followedBy,
    the bootstrap rules NamE, NumbeR, QuotE, SemI, GrouP, define and counter,
    the Operators registry and its UnaryOPS bin. Copied with their spellings
    unchanged, because a bin member or a literal leaf is its spelling: break,
    continue and return in BRANCHEs; loopOnAttributes and loopOnMembers in
    LOOPRESTRICt. Term labels (stuff, pound, leftCurly and the keyword terms
    such as if and while) are not rules and keep their names.
    The list of shared names, with the collision report, is in the plan of
    record, IncantForms/WorkingOn/macros.
*****************************************************************************/
// makeInstruct lives in Utilities, which every reader of this file searches: a name defined in KantGrammar is not reachable as an action (SEQ 494, measured)
registry(Utilities);
define
    // makeInstruct builds one instruct from a field carrying two entries, the op and its operand, binds testOP and returns it; it clears its pieces, so the next build starts empty (SEQ 478 R0). Each rule keeps its own missing-part check (Tony, SEQ 494 R1)
    // plainReturn the return copy carries the method binding, so the instruct comes back still bound to testOP (Tony, SEQ 497)
    makeInstruct code={
        miOp    := argument[1];
        miOpnd  := argument[2];
        instruct    +% *miOp;
        instruct    +% *miOpnd;
        instruct.methoD = "testOP";
        target      := copyOf(instruct);
        clear(instruct);
        clear(argument);
        return  *target;
        };
    // makePrint builds one print instruct from a field carrying two entries -- the print items and the sink's op -- and returns it: the walk PRINt, CERr and COUt share; only the op differs, and the op is the sink (SEQ 496)
    makePrint code={
        mpStuff := argument[1];
        mpOp    := argument[2];
        cur := *mpStuff.firsT;
        // threeKinds an item is an EXPRESSIOn's value, a shortcut (SHORTCUt's action marked it) or a format (it carries formatTYPE); the sink's op tells them apart
        while cur.datA != 0;
            // refusedItem an item whose EXPRESSIOn refused (it cleared its label) arrives as an empty PRINTXp: it prints nothing, as a refusing C++ operator's NULL prints nothing (SEQ 468)
            skipIt = 0;
            if *cur.taG == "PRINTXp";
                if *cur.listLengtH == 0;    skipIt = 1;
            // adjacentWalk an ADJACENt item is a run of adjacent operands (EXPRESSIOn's arm): each member is its own print item, known by the tag, never a C++ flag (SEQ 468)
            isAdj = 0;
            if *cur.taG == "ADJACENt";  isAdj = 1;
            if isAdj == 1;
                m := *cur.firsT;
                while m.datA != 0;
                    printItems +% *m;
                    m := *m.nexT;
            if skipIt == 0 && isAdj == 0;   printItems +% *cur;
            cur := *cur.nexT;
        // itemsMint the instruct takes a mint of the item list, never the local itself: a local is per-activation scratch, so every PRINt shared it and printed the last one's items (bear-trap 50, SEQ 446 S1)
        itemsMint   := copyOf(printItems);
        miArgs      +% mpOp itemsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        clear(argument);
        return  *built;
        };
    ;
registry(KantGrammar);
define
    KantGrammar isRule;
    EXPRESSIOn guard="^,;{}[]`";
    STATEMENt guard=[{A-Za-z0-9+-;(];
    ELSe;

    // oneRule the + on the label makes the set match a run, one SHORTCUt per run, as [set]+ did; no two-character refusal on the new road: `,` separates a shortcut from an operator (Tony, SEQ 469)
    SHORTCUt        shortcut+=[-+~`$_:,];
    ANYSTRINg=[^ \n\r\t;]+;
    BLOCk           leftCurly="{"- STATEMENt+ rightCurly="}"-;
    BRANCHEs bin
        break;
        continue;
        return;
    // dotIsASet a quoted '.' resolves to the . operator, whose guard is null, and the guard copy crashed (measured SEQ 449); digits one-or-more, as flags
    PRECISIOn       dot=[.] precision?=[0-9]+;
    // flagsOneOrMore a set term that matches zero characters fails the rule, so flags is optional one-or-more, never zero-or-more (measured SEQ 449)
    FORMAt          pound="#" flags?=[-# 0+']+ formatWIDTH?=NumbeR PRECISIOn? formatTYPE=[a-zA-Z] TOKENize;
    PRINTXp
        FORMAt;
        SHORTCUt;
        EXPRESSIOn;
    ITERSOURCe      UnaryOPS? ANYtoken;
    INVOKEd         startBox=[([] EXPRESSIOn endParen-?=")" endBrace-?="]";
    INVOKEr         ANYtoken INVOKEd;
    ANYORNUm
        NumbeR;
        ANYtoken;
    TOKEn
        INVOKEr;
        ANYtoken;
        QuotE;
        NumbeR;
        Operators;
        // keywordLast a Keywords entry fails ANYtoken; this last alternative lets it reach EXPRESSIOn, which refuses it by name (SEQ 579)
        KEYWORd;
    KEYWORd         NamE;
    // stringParked STRINGXp is out of TOKEn and parked (Tony, SEQ 449 R1, R2): after an expression it took a following `#5d` as a string, so a format there could not reach FORMAt
    STRINGXp        pound-="#" stuff=PRINTXp+ ;
    EXPRESSIOn      lizted=TOKEn+;
    SCOPEFIELd
        ANYtoken;
        NumbeR;
    LOOPEr=ANYtoken;
    LOOPRESTRICt
        loopOnAttributes="attributes";
        loopOnMembers="members";
    // noDefer the new road only builds, so no child waits for its parent: defer is off If, BASICELSe, Do, WHILe and FOr (Tony, SEQ 473 R0), and off BLOCk and BRANCh (SEQ 476 R2), and off ITERATe (SEQ 486 R2), and off ELSEIf, PRINt, CERr, COUt, SCOPEXp, XPRESs and STRINGXp (Tony, offline 2026-10-30; fleet unmoved); ELSEIf builds its own instruct (SEQ 491)
    ELSe
        BASICELSe   else- followedBy STATEMENt@;
        ELSEIf      op="ifThen" or- EXPRESSIOn SemI- STATEMENt ELSe?;
    WARDEd
        DEBUg       debug- followedBy rules?=NamE+ SemI-;
        DEf         def-="define" followedBy define^;
        Do          op="doLoop" do- followedBy STATEMENt while- EXPRESSIOn SemI-;
        If          op="ifThen" if- followedBy EXPRESSIOn SemI- STATEMENt ELSe?;
        FOr         for- followedBy LOOPEr in- reversE?="<-" EXPRESSIOn SemI- LOOPRESTRICt? STATEMENt;
        WHILe       op="whileLoop" while- followedBy EXPRESSIOn SemI-? STATEMENt;
        PRINt       print- followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PRINTXp+ SemI- ;
        CERr        cerr- followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PRINTXp+ SemI- ;
        COUt        cout- followedBy PRINTing- stuff=PRINTXp+ SemI- ;
        SEARCh      search- followedBy GrouP+ SemI-;
        BRANCh      BRANCHEs EXPRESSIOn? SemI-;
        SCOPEXp     colon-=":" scopeList=SCOPEFIELd+ SemI- ;
    XPRESs          EXPRESSIOn SemI- ;
    STATEMENt
        SemI;
        BLOCk;
        WARDEd;
        ITERATe     iterate- ANYtoken attributes? members? on- ITERSOURCe;
        XPRESs;
    STARt STATEMENt-+;
    // fileTop the new road's top level runs one statement at a time: TOPSTATEMENt runs each as it is built; STARt stays build-only (Tony, SEQ 495)
    TOPSTATEMENt    STATEMENt;
    // plusNotMinus FILe takes `+`, not STARt's `-+`: with the noLabel `-` the child's action never fires (measured SEQ 495)
    FILe            TOPSTATEMENt+;
    /*****************************************************************************
        kantGrammar actions. The actions for the rules defined above are added
        here as definition extensions. Doing it this way allows a quick view of
        each rule above without action code getting in the way.
    *****************************************************************************/
    INVOKEr code={
        if *INVOKEd.listLengtH;
            :INVOKEd startBox EXPRESSIOn;
            arg     = *EXPRESSIOn;
            if *startBox == "(";    op := Operators["call"];
            else                    op := Operators["=["];
            instruct    +% *op;
            instruct    +% unWrap(ANYtoken);
            instruct    +% *arg;
            // oneCallOneNode the call is copied INTO the label and the local cleared: returned by identity, the local was one node for every call built, so each ran the last callee (SEQ 510)
            // no method here: a rule's return RUNS a node that has one (bear-trap 34) -- EXPRESSIOn binds it on arrival
            icall       := copyOf(instruct);
            clear(instruct);
            *actionLabel   := *icall;
            return  *actionLabel;
        return ANYtoken;
        };
    // STARt only builds (SEQ 446): one seq instruct, op seq, its operand a mint of the list of statement instructs; testOP runs it
    STARt code={
        cur := *actionLabel.firsT;
        while cur.datA != 0;
            stmts +% *cur;
            cur := *cur.nexT;
        op := Operators["seq"];
        stmtsMint   := copyOf(stmts);
        miArgs      +% op stmtsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // SHORTCUt only builds (Shape S, Tony SEQ 461): one instruct, op printShortcut, its operand a copy of the matched text; opPrintList runs it
    SHORTCUt code={
        :actionLabel shortcut;
        scText      := copyOf(*shortcut);
        op := Operators["printShortcut"];
        miArgs      +% op scText;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // PRINt only builds (rung 1, Tony 2026-10-27): its sink is printList, stdout or printTO's divert; makePrint builds it
    PRINt code={
        op := Operators["printList"];
        mpArgs      +% stuff op;
        built       := makePrint(mpArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // ITERATe only builds (SEQ 486 R2): one instruct, op iterateOn, its operand a mint of [cursor, source, attributes 0/1, members 0/1]; opIterate makes the cursor an iterator when it RUNS (iterateSetup, shared with the old road)
    ITERATe code={
        :actionLabel ANYtoken attributes members ITERSOURCe;
        cursor  := **ANYtoken;
        // filterSlots the filters ride as two counts in fixed places, so opIterate reads them by position
        aOn = 0;
        if attributes.datA != 0;    aOn = 1;
        mOn = 0;
        if members.datA != 0;       mOn = 1;
        :ITERSOURCe ANYtoken;
        source  := **ANYtoken;
        // sourceStar a star on the source is built, never dropped: the source becomes a deref instruct, which opIterate runs (2026-11-05)
        if *ITERSOURCe["UnaryOPS"];
            derefOp     := Operators["deref"];
            miArgs      +% derefOp source;
            source      := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        // childRefused a cursor or source that handed up nothing refuses by name
        if cursor.datA == 0;
            cerr "REFUSED ITERATe -- its cursor handed up nothing":;
            clear(*actionLabel);
            return *actionLabel;
        if source.datA == 0;
            cerr "REFUSED ITERATe -- its source handed up nothing":;
            clear(*actionLabel);
            return *actionLabel;
        iterArms    +% *cursor;
        iterArms    +% *source;
        aMint       := copyOf(aOn);
        mMint       := copyOf(mOn);
        iterArms    +% *aMint;
        iterArms    +% *mMint;
        op := Operators["iterateOn"];
        armsMint    := copyOf(iterArms);
        miArgs      +% op armsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // FOr only builds (SEQ 482 R0): one instruct, op forLoop, its operand a mint of [cursor, source, statement] and the reverse mark when `<-` is present; opFor binds the cursor to each member when the loop RUNS
    FOr code={
        :actionLabel LOOPEr EXPRESSIOn LOOPRESTRICt STATEMENt reversE;
        // restrictParked attributes / members is not built on the new road yet (SEQ 482 R1)
        if LOOPRESTRICt.datA != 0;
            cerr "REFUSED FOr attributes / members -- not built on the new road yet":;
            clear(*actionLabel);
            return *actionLabel;
        cursor  := **LOOPEr;
        source  := **EXPRESSIOn;
        body    := **STATEMENt;
        // bodyRefused a statement that handed up no instruct refuses by name, as WHILe's (SEQ 476 R6)
        bodyM   := *body.methoD;
        if bodyM == 0;
            cerr "REFUSED FOr -- its statement handed up no instruct":;
            clear(*actionLabel);
            return *actionLabel;
        forArms +% *cursor;
        forArms +% *source;
        forArms +% *body;
        // reverseMark `<-` rides as a fourth operand; its presence is the meaning
        if reversE.datA != 0;
            revMark := copyOf(*reversE);
            forArms +% *revMark;
        op := Operators["forLoop"];
        armsMint    := copyOf(forArms);
        miArgs      +% op armsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        // ownsItsBody the loop walks DOWN its own body and rebinds each seq it owns to bodySeq, so a break there passes up to it (SEQ 516)
        loopOwnsBody(*built);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // OPERATOr the default action (SEQ 580): a rule declared with op= builds one instruct, op the declared Operators entry, operand a mint of its label's entries in order
    OPERATOr code={
        opR := ruleOpOf(*actionLabel);
        // noOp ruleOpOf refused by name: nothing is built
        if opR.datA == 0;
            clear(*actionLabel);
            return *actionLabel;
        ruleT := *actionLabel.taG;
        slot := *actionLabel.firsT;
        while slot.datA != 0;
            slN := *slot.taG;
            sv := **slot;
            // slotNothing any slot that handed up nothing refuses by its own label
            if sv.datA == 0;
                cerr "REFUSED" ruleT "-- its" slN "handed up nothing":;
                clear(*actionLabel);
                return *actionLabel;
            // slotStatement a STATEMENt slot must hand up an instruct
            if *slot.taG == "STATEMENt";
                svM := *sv.methoD;
                if svM == 0;
                    cerr "REFUSED" ruleT "-- its" slN "handed up no instruct":;
                    clear(*actionLabel);
                    return *actionLabel;
            opArms  +% *sv;
            slot := *slot.nexT;
        armsMint    := copyOf(opArms);
        miArgs      +% opR armsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        // loopTrait an op carrying ownsBody walks down its own body and rebinds each seq it owns to bodySeq (loopOwnsBody, SEQ 516)
        if *opR["ownsBody"];    loopOwnsBody(*built);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // whileIsAnOperator WHILe declares op= on its grammar line and takes the default action (SEQ 580)
    WHILe ruleMethod=OPERATOr;
    // doIsAnOperator Do declares op= and takes the default action; its slots in order are the statement then the condition, the order opDo reads (SEQ 591)
    Do ruleMethod=OPERATOr;
    // ifIsAnOperator If and ELSEIf declare op="ifThen" and take the default action; the slots in order are the condition, the then statement and the else, absent when absent, the order opIf reads (SEQ 592)
    If ruleMethod=OPERATOr;
    ELSEIf ruleMethod=OPERATOr;
    // BRANCh only builds (SEQ 476 R0): break and continue build one instruct, op branchSignal, its operand a copy of the keyword; opBranch sets the signal
    // returnArms return builds the same instruct, its operand a mint of [keyword, value?]; opBranch runs the value, sets kind 3 and hands the value up (SEQ 508 / 509)
    BRANCh code={
        :actionLabel BRANCHEs EXPRESSIOn;
        kw := **BRANCHEs;
        op := Operators["branchSignal"];
        kwMint      := copyOf(*kw);
        if *kw.taG == "return";
            retArms +% *kwMint;
            xLen    := *EXPRESSIOn.listLengtH;
            if xLen != 0;
                val     := **EXPRESSIOn;
                retArms +% *val;
            armsMint    := copyOf(retArms);
            miArgs      +% op armsMint;
        if *kw.taG != "return";
            miArgs      +% op kwMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // bridgeRun a C++-only statement runs its C++ rule method at RUN, in its place (SEQ 489, bridge-run): one instruct, op runRule, its operand a mint of the statement's label bound to that method; opRunRule runs it
    SEARCh code={
        lMint       := copyOf(*actionLabel);
        *lMint.methoD = "aCTionSearch";
        op := Operators["runRule"];
        miArgs      +% op lMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    DEBUg code={
        lMint       := copyOf(*actionLabel);
        *lMint.methoD = "aCTionDEBUG";
        op := Operators["runRule"];
        miArgs      +% op lMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    SCOPEXp code={
        lMint       := copyOf(*actionLabel);
        *lMint.methoD = "aCTionScopeXP";
        op := Operators["runRule"];
        miArgs      +% op lMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // CERr builds as PRINt does (SEQ 496): its sink is cerrList, stderr
    CERr code={
        op := Operators["cerrList"];
        mpArgs      +% stuff op;
        built       := makePrint(mpArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // COUt builds as PRINt does (SEQ 496): its sink is coutList, stdout and never printTO's divert (KANT-23)
    COUt code={
        op := Operators["coutList"];
        mpArgs      +% stuff op;
        built       := makePrint(mpArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // BLOCk only builds (SEQ 476 R1): one seq instruct, as STARt's, its operand a mint of the list of statement instructs
    BLOCk code={
        cur := *actionLabel.firsT;
        while cur.datA != 0;
            blockStmts +% *cur;
            cur := *cur.nexT;
        op := Operators["seq"];
        stmtsMint   := copyOf(blockStmts);
        miArgs      +% op stmtsMint;
        built       := makeInstruct(miArgs);
        *actionLabel   := *built;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    // TOPSTATEMENt runs its statement as soon as it is built (SEQ 495), so a define or an include lands between its neighbours
    TOPSTATEMENt code={
        :actionLabel STATEMENt;
        ts := **STATEMENt;
        topRun = 1;
        // topBranch a break or continue at the top level has no loop or block to end: it refuses by name and never runs, so no signal is left set; return builds as branchSignal since SEQ 508 and refuses here too (A2, Tony, SEQ 495)
        tsL := *ts.listLengtH;
        if tsL != 0;
            tsOp := *ts[1];
            if *tsOp.taG == "branchSignal";
                kw := *ts[2];
                // returnNamesItself return's operand is the mint [keyword, value?], so the refusal names its first entry (SEQ 509)
                kwL := *kw.listLengtH;
                if kwL != 0;
                    k1 := *kw[1];
                    if *k1.taG == "return";     kw := *k1;
                kwT := *kw.taG;
                cerr "REFUSED" kwT "-- at the top level there is no loop or block to end":;
                topRun = 0;
        // unlandedRefused a signal still set when the statement finishes reached the top level unlanded: refuseTopSignal refuses it by name and clears it (SEQ 516)
        if topRun == 1;     refuseTopSignal(*ts);
        return *actionLabel;
        };
    // XPRESs hands its EXPRESSIOn's instruct up as the statement, so STARt's seq runs it (SQ-4)
    XPRESs code={
        :actionLabel EXPRESSIOn;
        // twoStars EXPRESSIOn is the child label, which holds the instruct: one star hands opSeq the label, and opSeq runs only an instruct, skipping the rest silently
        xi := **EXPRESSIOn;
        *actionLabel := *xi;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    EXPRESSIOn code={
        cur := *lizted.firsT;
        prevOp = 1;
        npre = 0;
        while cur.datA != 0;
            // keywordRefused a keyword inside an expression refuses by name and ends the statement: a keyword form is not an expression yet (SEQ 579 R1)
            // keywordShape KEYWORd arrives as a TOKEn whose first entry is its NamE; no other child has that shape
            if *cur.taG == "TOKEn";
                if *cur.listLengtH != 0;
                    kwF := *cur.firsT;
                    if *kwF.taG == "NamE";
                        kwT := *kwF.texT;
                        cerr "REFUSED" kwT "-- a keyword inside an expression; a keyword form is not an expression yet":;
                        clear(*actionLabel);
                        return *actionLabel;
            if *cur.instructTypE != 0;
                // prefixRun a run of prefixes is counted; the operand wraps them innermost first (SEQ 571 R0)
                if prevOp == 1;
                    pre = cur;
                    npre = npre + 1;
                else
                    op      = cur;
                    // tiers (SEQ 434): 1 arithmetic folds at once; 2 compare, 3 logic, 4 assign each wait in their own pending slot
                    kind    = 1;
                    if *cur["compareTier"];     kind = 2;
                    if *cur["shortCircuit"];    kind = 3;
                    if *cur["assignTier"];      kind = 4;
                prevOp = 1;
            else
                prevOp = 0;
                wrapped = 0;
                if pre.datA != 0;
                    // prefixesNest walk back from the operand over the counted run: the nearest prefix wraps first, so - - qv reads 3 (SEQ 571 R0)
                    opnd := *cur;
                    pp := *cur.prioR;
                    while npre > 0;
                        pOp = pp;
                        if *pOp.taG == "-";     pOp := Operators["negate"];
                        or *pOp.taG == "*";     pOp := Operators["deref"];
                        instruct    +% pOp opnd;
                        // an instruct is known by its method: testOP runs it (SEQ 428)
                        instruct.methoD = "testOP";
                        opnd        := copyOf(instruct);
                        clear(instruct);
                        clear(pOp);
                        pp := *pp.prioR;
                        npre = npre - 1;
                    clear(pre);
                    wrapped = 1;
                else    opnd = cur;
                // INVOKEr's call arrives as an instruct without its method; it gets it here, never at INVOKEr's return
                if *cur.taG == "instruct";  *cur.methoD = "testOP";
                // callRefused a call or subscript whose argument is a run (INVOKEd's EXPRESSIOn handed up an ADJACENt list) refuses by name, as an operator does (SEQ 468 R0)
                if *cur.taG == "instruct";
                    cArg := *cur[3];
                    if *cArg.taG == "ADJACENt";
                        cOp := *cur[1];
                        adjOp := *cOp.taG;
                        cerr "REFUSED" adjOp "-- a list of adjacent operands is not built on the new road yet":;
                        clear(*actionLabel);
                        return *actionLabel;
                // adjacentRun an operand followed by an operand, no op between, joins a run; the run ends at an operator or the end and becomes ONE operand, an ADJACENt list (SEQ 468)
                nx := *cur.nexT;
                nxOpnd = 0;
                if nx.datA != 0;
                    if *nx.instructTypE == 0;   nxOpnd = 1;
                // wrappedJoins a prefixed operand joins as its prefix instruct, so - qa inside a run keeps its - (SEQ 468 R3)
                if nxOpnd == 1;
                    if wrapped == 1;    ADJACENt +% *opnd;
                    else                ADJACENt +% *cur;
                    cur := *nx;
                    continue;
                if ADJACENt.listLengtH != 0;
                    if wrapped == 1;    ADJACENt +% *opnd;
                    else                ADJACENt +% *cur;
                    opnd := copyOf(ADJACENt);
                    clear(ADJACENt);
                    // adjacentRefused a list meeting ANY operator, either side, refuses by name: list operators are parked, not forbidden (SEQ 467, SEQ 468 R0)
                    adjRef = 0;
                    if op.datA != 0;    adjRef = 1;
                    if nx.datA != 0;    adjRef = 2;
                    if adjRef == 1;     adjOp := *op.taG;
                    if adjRef == 2;     adjOp := *nx.taG;
                    if adjRef != 0;
                        cerr "REFUSED" adjOp "-- a list of adjacent operands is not built on the new road yet":;
                        clear(*actionLabel);
                        return *actionLabel;
                if target.datA == 0;    target = opnd;
                else    arg = opnd;
                if op.datA != 0 && target.datA != 0;
                    if arg.datA != 0;
                        if kind == 1;
                            instruct    +% op target arg;
                            instruct.methoD = "testOP";
                            target      := copyOf(instruct);
                        else
                            // chainRefused a comparison arriving while one is pending is a chain (a < b < c): refused by name, the old road's wording, never folded as (a < b) < c (SEQ 570 R0)
                            if kind == 2;
                                if cmpP.datA != 0;
                                    chTop := *cmpP[1];
                                    chOp := *chTop.taG;
                                    cerr "REFUSED" chOp "-- a chained comparison (a < b < c) -- refused by name (SEQ 318 R1)":;
                                    clear(*actionLabel);
                                    return *actionLabel;
                            // a looser operator first closes every tighter slot into the target; an equal one closes its own, so a tier reads left to right; assign never closes assign
                            if kind >= 2;
                                if cmpP.datA != 0;
                                    *cmpP   +% *target;
                                    target  = cmpP;
                                    clear(cmpP);
                            if kind >= 3;
                                if logP.datA != 0;
                                    *logP   +% *target;
                                    target  = logP;
                                    clear(logP);
                            // instructTarget everything left of = is the target: an instruct target whose top op is not on the field allow-list is refused by name at build (Tony, SEQ 442 R1, SEQ 443 R1')
                            if kind == 4;
                                if *target.taG == "instruct";
                                    tTop := *target[1];
                                    tTopT := *tTop.taG;
                                    // fieldOps subscript, star, dot and the three by-name lookups hand back a field; every other top op, current or future, is refused
                                    tOk = 0;
                                    if *tTop.taG == "=[";       tOk = 1;
                                    if *tTop.taG == "deref";    tOk = 1;
                                    if *tTop.taG == ".";        tOk = 1;
                                    if *tTop.taG == "=%";       tOk = 1;
                                    if *tTop.taG == "=/";       tOk = 1;
                                    if *tTop.taG == "=<";       tOk = 1;
                                    if tOk == 0;
                                        asgOp := *op.taG;
                                        cerr "REFUSED" asgOp "-- the left of" asgOp "is an instruct, not a field: its op is" tTopT:;
                                        clear(*actionLabel);
                                        return *actionLabel;
                            instruct +% op target;
                            instruct    = kind;
                            instruct.methoD = "testOP";
                            if kind == 2;   cmpP := copyOf(instruct);
                            if kind == 3;   logP := copyOf(instruct);
                            // a second assign nests: it becomes the pending one's argument, completed at the end; asgO keeps the outermost
                            if kind == 4;
                                if asgP.datA != 0;
                                    newA    := copyOf(instruct);
                                    *asgP   +% *newA;
                                    if asgO.datA == 0;  asgO = asgP;
                                    asgP    = newA;
                                else    asgP := copyOf(instruct);
                            target = arg;
                    clear(instruct);
                    clear(arg);
                    clear(op);
            cur := *cur.nexT;
        // the end closes the slots tightest first
        if cmpP.datA != 0;
            *cmpP +% *target;
            target = cmpP;
        if logP.datA != 0;
            *logP +% *target;
            target = logP;
        if asgP.datA != 0;
            *asgP +% *target;
            target = asgP;
            if asgO.datA != 0;  target = asgO;
        // the chain goes INTO this rule's label, so a caller (INVOKEd, or a command line) gets the label back
        *actionLabel := *target;
        return  *actionLabel;
        };
    ;
bail();

/***************************************************************************
    Comments
***************************************************************************/

EXPRESSIOn, in prose (Tony's original, Clod's improvements; written by Clay and
Tony, read against the action and corrected by Clod, SEQ 562, 2026-11-08). The
code is the authority; each correction is marked CORRECTED and says what the
code does.

EXPRESSIOn's grammar hands the action one flat list, lizted: the tokens of
the expression in source order, operands and operators side by side. The
action walks that list once, left to right, and builds one instruct chain.
The chain goes into the rule's own label (actionLabel), so whoever called
EXPRESSIOn gets the label back holding the chain.

Telling an operator from an operand. A token with an instructTypE is an
operator; anything else is an operand. One flag, prevOp, remembers whether
the last token was an operator. It starts as 1, as if an operator had come
before the first token. So an operator that follows an operator, or opens
the expression, is a PREFIX. An operator that follows an operand is BINARY.

Prefixes. A prefix waits in pre until its operand arrives. A - becomes
Operators["negate"] and a * becomes Operators["deref"]; any other prefix
stays as it is. When the operand comes, the two are wrapped into an
instruct, prefix then operand, and that instruct stands in for the operand
from then on.
    CORRECTED: only ONE prefix waits. pre = cur replaces whatever pre held,
    so of two prefixes in a row only the second is kept: - - qv reads -3 on
    this road, 3 on the old road (measured 2026-11-08).

Calls. A call arrives from INVOKEr as an instruct with no method; it gets
testOP here. If its argument is a run of side-by-side operands, the call is
refused by name.
    CORRECTED: calls AND subscripts. INVOKEr builds both, op call for ( and
    op =[ for [ , and the action treats every operand tagged instruct alike:
    it binds testOP, and refuses one whose third entry (the argument) is an
    ADJACENt run, naming the op.

Side-by-side operands. Two operands with no operator between them start a
run. The run collects operands until an operator or the end of the list,
then becomes ONE operand, an ADJACENt list. If an operator touches the run
on either side, the expression is refused by name: list operators are
parked, not forbidden.

The tiers. Every binary operator has a tier, read from its entry in
setup's Operators:
    1 arithmetic   (no tier flag)
    2 compare      (compareTier)
    3 logic        (shortCircuit)
    4 assign       (assignTier)
The walk keeps a running value, target. The first operand becomes target;
each later one is arg, the right side of the operator before it.

Arithmetic folds at once. When an arithmetic operator has both sides, it is
built straight away and the result becomes target. So arithmetic reads
strictly left to right, with no precedence among its own operators:
a + b * 2 is (a + b) * 2.

Looser tiers wait. A compare, logic or assign operator does not fold at
once. It is built with only its left side and parked in its tier's pending
slot: cmpP for compare, logP for logic, asgP for assign. target then starts
again from the operator's right side, so everything tighter to its right
gathers first.

Closing a slot. When a new compare, logic or assign operator arrives, any
pending slot at its own tier or tighter is closed first: the slot takes
target as its right side and becomes target. That is why a looser operator
waits for the tighter ones, and why two operators of the same tier read
left to right (a < b < c is (a < b) < c). Assign never closes assign.
    CORRECTED, timing only: nothing happens when the operator itself
    arrives; it is held in op. The closing, the building and the parking all
    happen when its RIGHT operand arrives, and the right operand becomes
    target. The results are the same.

Chained assign. A second = nests inside the first as its right side, so
a = b = c is a = (b = c). asgO remembers the outermost one.

What may sit left of =. If the left side of an assign is itself an instruct,
its top operator must hand back a field: subscript =[ , star deref, dot,
or the by-name lookups =% =/ =< . Any other top operator, present or
future, is refused by name when the chain is built.

The end. When the list runs out, the pending slots close tightest first:
compare, then logic, then assign. The outermost assign, if there is one,
is the answer.

A worked example: r = a + b * 2 > lim && ok
    r               target is r
    =               assign, waits: asgP = [= r]; target starts again
    a + b           arithmetic folds: target = [+ a b]
    * 2             folds again: target = [* [+ a b] 2]
    > lim           compare, waits: cmpP = [> that]; target = lim
    && ok           logic arrives: cmpP closes, target = [> ... lim];
                    logP = [&& that]; target = ok
    end             logP closes: [&& [> ...] ok]
                    asgP closes: [= r [&& [> [* [+ a b] 2] lim] ok]]
The label hands back that one chain. (By the timing correction above, asgP
is built when a arrives, cmpP when lim arrives, logP when ok arrives. Run on
this road with a=1 b=3 lim=5 ok=1, r reads 1.)

What it refuses, each by name, the action ending at once:
    a call or subscript whose argument is a side-by-side run
    a side-by-side run that touches any operator
    an instruct left of = whose top operator is not on the field list
"Ending at once" is: the action clears its label and returns it, so the
caller receives an empty label.

